# 
# Help File Format Information
# 
# Special characters at the beginning of each line determine
# the function of that line:
#       `#' = Comment line, ignored by program
#       `:' = Control line. the program looks for these
#             lines, and reads the following characters as a
#             help subject label. One special case is
#             ":page", which indicates a page break within a
#             help subject. 
# Maximum line length for help items is 61 chrs. Longer lines
# are truncated. Maximum number of lines/screen is 16
# (additional lines are automatically moved to the next
# screen).
# 
#-----------------------------------------------------------
#
:chedit_prich
       [Priority Channel Selection/Unhide Channel Data]

 Use the up and down arrow keys to highlight a Priority
 Channel for scanning, and press the Space Bar.  

 If the channel was hidden, it will be unhidden. Unless it
 was already selected, it will be selected as the Priority 
 Channel ("[]" around it). If it was already selected, it 
 will be deselected and there will be NO Priority Channel.
 A new selection supercedes any previous selection.

 Select another field with the arrow keys when done.

 NOTE: Dual Watch and Priority selection cannot both be
 enabled (select in Common Data).
#
#
#
:chedit_name
                     [Naming a Channel]

 Use the up and down arrows to highlight a channel to name.
 Then use up to 4 characters: "0 - 9", "A - Z", "< = > * - \
 / _" and space(s) to enter a Name for the highlighted
 channel. While entering, the highlighting is replaced by a
 blinking cursor. Backspace deletes one character.

 Press Enter to accept, or Esc to cancel.

 NOTE: DTMF SelCall and Channel Names cannot both be enabled
 (select in Common Data).
#
#
#
:chedit_dtmf
                 [Setting DTMF SelCall Codes]

 Use the up and down arrows to highlight a channel in which 
 to program a 3-digit DTMF SelCall. Then press 0 - 9 to
 enter (exactly 3) digits. Backspace deletes, Esc cancels. 

 After entering the digits, you can press the Space Bar to
 toggle the Transpond function on ("T" suffix displayed),
 and again to disable the DMTF SelCall on this channel.

 When set as desired, press Enter to accept the displayed
 settings.
 
 NOTE: DTMF SelCall and Channel Names cannot both be enabled
 (select in Common Data).

#
#
#
:chedit_rxf
   [Edit Rx/Simplex Frequency & Hide/Unhide Channel Data]

 Use 0 - 9 to enter the desired channel frequency, and press
 Enter. This frequency will be adjusted if it does not match
 the Channel Step parameter (in Environ Data), and will also
 copy Rx Freq + Default Split(in Environ Data) to Tx Freq
 field. You do not need to enter all 8 digits: empty digits
 to the right are zero-filled. Pressing [.] (period) after
 several digits forces them to MHz. Press period first to
 change only kHz.

 Pressing only the Space Bar without entering a number
 toggles the data for the entire channel between hidden and
 unhidden (except channels 1-01, which cannot be hidden).
 Hidden channels display "--" in place of field entries,
 and are not used for operation (although they are still
#
:page
#

 stored in hidden form for possible recall later).

 NOTE! Any entries or changes to this field will also occur
       in the Tx Freq. field, so you may need to edit that
       field after making any changes here.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                Frequency).
 Space Bar:  Hide/Unhide entire channel (exc. 1-01 & 2-01).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:chedit_cdec
 [Toggle Subaudible CTCSS Decoder, and set CTCSS frequency]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the decoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Tone Select window, from which you may
 select a frequency with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the CTCSS frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys. When the
 desired CTCSS frequency is displayed, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones),
 the Tone Select Window will appear as described above, with
 the nearest valid frequency pre-selected.

 Turning on CTCSS turns off DCS (both cannot be on).
 Turning off CTCSS also turns off BTLO (tone) if it was on.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
#
#
#
:chedit_ddec
          [Toggle DCS Decoder, and set DCS Code]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the decoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Code Select window, from which you may
 select a DCS Code with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the 3-digit DCS Code you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 keys. When the desired Code is
 displayed, press Enter to accept. If the Code was invalid
 (not among the standard Code numbers), the Code Select
 window will appear as described above, with the nearest
 valid Code pre-selected.

 Turning on DCS turns off CTCSS (both cannot be on).
 Turning off DCS also turns off BTLO (tone), if it was on.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 7:      Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_tone
    [2-Tone Decoder On/Off Toggle & Frequency Selection]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the 2-Tone Decoder on/off, or
 press Enter to display the 2-Tone Select window, from which
 you may select a 2-Tone frequency with the Arrow Keys
 (pressing Enter again to accept, or Esc to cancel). 

 If you know the 2-Tone frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] keys. When the desired fre-
 quency is displayed, press Enter to accept. If the frequency
 was invalid (not among the standard frequencies), the 2-Tone
 Select window will appear as described above, with the
 nearest valid frequency pre-selected.
#
:page
#
 
 Note: A and B tones toggle together - you cannot activate
 only one. Also, Burst and 2-Tone cannot both be enabled
 (in Common Data).

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in 2-Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9 & [.]: Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display 2-Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.

 Select 2-Tone Decoder Group in Common Data.
#
#
#
:chedit_scan
                 [Scan Stop/Skip Toggle]

 If scanning is enabled in the Common Data, the Space Bar
 selects whether this channel will be included in scanning
 (stop), or be skipped over.

 If "שששש" is displayed in this field, scanning is disabled
 in the Common Data, and no selection is possible unless
 Common Data is first edited to enable scanning.
#
#
#
:chedit_save
                [Power Saver on/off toggle]

Press the Space Bar to toggle the automatic Power Saver
feature on and off. When on, the transceiver draws less
current from the battery by turning itself off periodically
during squelched monitoring on this channel, to prolong
battery charge life. The Saving Interval (off time) can be
set in the Common Data.

Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.

NOTE! If "ששש" appears in the Power Save field, Power Saving
      has been disabled (in Common Data). You must edit the
      Common Data to enable it before setting this field.
#
#
#
:chedit_txf
  [Enter Transmit Frequency & Enable/Disable Transmission]

Use keys 0 - 9 to enter the desired transmitting frequency,
and press the Enter key to accept. If the frequency does not
match the Channel Step parameter (in Common Data), it will be
adjusted to the nearest valid step. You do not need to enter
all 8 digits: empty digits to the right are zero-filled.

Pressing [.] (period) after several digits forces them to
MHz, while pressing [.] first allows entering only kHz.

Pressing only the Space Bar without entering a number toggles
the this channel's transmit capability on and off (when off,
previously set transmitting parameters are hidden until the
transmitter is again enabled for this channel, and "Tx Inh"
is displayed).
#
#
#
:page
#

NOTE! The Tx Freq is automatically copied from the Rx Freq,
      so any changes here will be LOST if the Rx Freq is
      changed. Therefore, the Rx Freq should be set first,
      before editing the Tx Freq. 

Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after Editing Freq.)
Space Bar:  Enable/Disable Transmission on this channel.
0 - 9:      Input a digit.
[.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits to MHz, or next
                digits to kHz.
Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:chedit_cenc
#
   [Toggle Tone Squelch Encoder, and set CTCSS frequency]

 Press Space to toggle the Encoder on/off, or press Enter to
 display the Tone Select window (if not viewing it already),
 from which you may select a frequency with the Arrow Keys
 (pressing Enter again to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the CTCSS frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys (entering
 a number or [.] forces the Encoder on). When the display
 shows the desired CTCSS frequency, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones)
 the Tone Select Window will appear as mentioned above.

 NOTE! if the channel is hidden when you make an entry, it
 will be unhidden. Edit the Rx Freq field to hide it again.
#
#
#
:page
#

 NOTE: Turning on the CTCSS Encoder automatically turns off
 the DCS encoder, if it was on.
 
 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_denc
#
       [Toggle DCS Encoder, and set DCS Encoder Code]

 Press Space to toggle the Encoder on/off, or press Enter to
 display the Code Select window (if not viewing it already),
 from which you may select a code with the Arrow Keys
 (pressing Enter again to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the DCS code you want, you can enter it directly
 with the 0 - 7 keys (entering a number forces the Encoder
 on). When the display shows the desired code, press Enter to
 accept. If the code was invalid (not among the standard
 codes) the Code Select Window will appear as mentioned
 above.

 NOTE! if the channel is hidden when you make an entry, it
 will be unhidden. Edit the Rx Freq field to hide it again.
#
#
#
:page
#

 NOTE: Turning on the DCS Encoder automatically turns off
 the CTCSS encoder, if it was on.
 
 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 7:      Direct code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_burst
    [Transmitter Tone Burst Frequency Selection/Enable]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Burst Encoder on/off, or
 press Enter to display the Burst Tone Select window, from
 which you may select a Burst frequency with the Arrow Keys
 (pressing Enter again to accept, or Esc to cancel). 

 If you know the Burst frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] keys. When the desired fre-
 quency is displayed, press Enter to accept. If the frequency
 was invalid (not among the standard frequencies), the Burst
 Tone Select window will appear as described above, with the
 nearest valid frequency pre-selected.

 Note: Burst and 2-Tone cannot both be enabled (in Common
 Data).
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9 & [.]: Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_bclo
    [Busy Channel/Tone Lock-Out: Carrier/Tone/Off Toggle]

 The Space Bar (or Backspace Key) toggles the BCLO/BTLO
 selection between Carrier Control (cw), CTCSS/DCS Control
 (tone), or off.

 Note: You must press Enter after making a selection.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (after pressing Enter).
 Space Bar:  Toggle BCLO mode.
# Space Bar, Backspace: Toggle BCLO mode.
#
#
#
:chedit_tpwr
#
              [Transmit Power high/low toggle]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the transmitter power between
 high and low. If transmission on this channel was disabled,
 it will now be enabled.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle high/low.
#
#
#
:chedit_tot
#
           [Transmit Time-Out Timer on/off toggle]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Transmit Time-Out Timer on
 and off. When on, the transmitting time on this channel is
 limited to the Tx Time-Out Time set in Common Data (1/2 to
 7-1/2 minutes).

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.

 NOTE! If "..." appears in the Tx Timer field, the Time-Out
       Timer has been disabled (in Common Data). You must
       edit the Common Data to enable it before setting this
       field.
#
#
#
:file_select
#
                   [File Selection Window]

 Select a filename (lower-case letters) or subdirectory
 (upper-case, in angle brackets <>) using the Arrow Keys, and
 then the Enter Key to accept your selection. If you selected
 a subdirectory, the window contents will be replaced with
 that subdirectory's contents, from which you can select
 again. You can also delete a file or subdirectory with [F4].

 You can also enter a new drive letter or filespec directly.
 Pressing alphanumeric keys activates the filespec line
 editor along the bottom of the window. You can use wildcards
 ("*" or "?") to display only certain files (eg.,
 :\CHDATA\*.CH and Enter displays only files with the "CH"
 extension in the CHDATA subdirectory on drive B). Tab
 returns you to the upper half of the selection window.
#
:page
#
 (at any time in Selection Window)
 Enter Key:  Accept selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel operation and return to Channel Editor.
 Tab Key:    Switch between Selection Window and line editor.

 (for selection in upper part of window)
 Arrow Keys: Select filename/subdirectory.
 Home/End:   Jump to first/last selection on screen.
 [F4] Key:   Delete current file selection from disk.
 PageDown:   Jump down one page (for large directories).
 PageUp:     Jump up one page (for large directories).

 (for filespec editor at bottom of window)
 Delete:     Delete character under cursor.
 Backspace:  Delete character to left.
 Insert:     Toggle insert/overwrite modes.
#
#
#
:path_edit
#
                 [File Selection Window]

 Select a filename (lower-case letters) or subdirectory
 (upper-case, in angle brackets <>) using the Arrow Keys, and
 then the Enter Key to accept your selection. If you selected
 a subdirectory, the window contents will be replaced with
 that subdirectory's contents, from which you can select
 again.  If you select a file, you can delete it with [F4].

 You can also enter a new drive letter or filespec directly.
 Pressing alphanumeric keys activates the filespec line
 editor along the bottom of the window. You can use wildcards
 ("*" or "?") to display only certain files (eg.,
 B:\CHDATA\*.CH and Enter displays only files with the "CH"
 extension in the CHDATA subdirectory on drive B. Tab returns
 you to the upper half of the selection window.
#
:page
#
 (at any time in Selection Window)
 Enter Key:  Accept selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel operation and return to Channel Editor.
 Tab Key:    Switch between Selection Window and line editor.

 (for selection in upper part of window)
 Arrow Keys: Select filename/subdirectory.
 Home/End:   Jump to first/last selection on screen.
 [F4] Key:   Delete current file selection from disk.
 PageDown:   Jump down one page (for large directories).
 PageUp:     Jump up one page (for large directories).

 (for filespec editor at bottom of window)
 Delete:     Delete character under cursor.
 Backspace:  Delete character to left.
 Insert:     Toggle insert/overwrite modes.
#
#
#
:read_rom
#
     [Transfer data between PC and Transceiver's EEPROM]

 The Space Bar is used to proceed with the transfer operation
 after ensuring the hardware is set up for the transfer. To
 cancel and return to the Channel Editor, press the Esc Key
 instead.

 The status of the transfer operation will be displayed when
 finished (a few seconds after pressing the Space Bar).

 NOTE! Press the Esc Key if the system appears to hang. Then
 make sure the power indicator on the interface box is on,
 and check the cables for a connection problem.
#
#
#
:write_rom
#
    [Transfer data between PC and Transceiver's EEPROM]

 The Space Bar is used to proceed with the transfer operation
 after ensuring the hardware is set up for the transfer. To
 cancel and return to the Channel Editor, press the Esc Key
 instead.

 The status of the transfer operation will be displayed when
 finished (a few seconds after pressing the Space Bar).

 NOTE! Press the Esc Key if the system appears to hang. Then
 make sure the power indicator on the interface box is on,
 and check the cables for a connection problem.
#
#
#
:edit_common
#
               [Edit Common Data Parameters]

 Use the Arrow Keys to select the parameter to edit. Then use
 the Space Bar or Backspace Key to toggle among all possible
 settings for that parameter. When all parameters are set as
 desired, press the Esc Key to return to the Channel Editor.

 Channel Scan:
 Enable/disable scanning

 Scan Resume:
 The "5s timer" selection causes scanning to resume after a 
 5-second pause. The "carrier" selection causes scanning to 
 resume after squelch closes.
#
:page
#

 User Scan Program:
 Enable/disable user selection of channels to scan.

 Dual Watch:
 Enable/disable dual watch (priority scanning).

 Power On Dual Watch:
 Activate dual watch whenever power switched on.

 Dual Watch Speed:
 Select slow or fast channel monitor toggling.
#
:page
#

 Channel after PTT:
 Select one of priority channel, home channel or the last
 busy channel to return to when PTT switch is pressed
 during scanning or priority monitoring (dual watch).

 Monitor:
 Enable/disable MON button (to override squelch).

 Beep:
 When enabled, pressing a key on the radio produces a
 beep in the loudspeaker.

 Lighting Lock:
 Enable/disable KEY LOCK button control over LCD lamp
 and red TX/BUSY indicator.
#
:page
#

 Power Save:
 Sets the "sleep/awake" ratio for the power save function.
 The "awake" time is either 30 or 300 ms (selected in hard-
 ware). The "sleep" time is 1 to 15 times the selected 
 "awake" time. Select "Disabled" if the power save function 
 is not desired.

 Tx Timer-Out: 
 Transmitter Time-Out Timer enabled (to or limit length of 
 transmissions) or disabled.

 Tx Timer-Out Time:
 Maximum length of transmission (30 sec, 1 min, 2 min or 3  
 min) when Tx Time-Out Timer (above) is enabled.
#
:page
#

 Time-Out Resume:
 Minimum time to wait (none, or 6, 20 or 60 sec) after Tx 
 Time-Out before transmitter can be reactivated.

 Squelch Tail Elimination:
 When the CTCSS option is installed and active and this
 feature is enabled, releasing the PTT switch will leave
 the transmitter active (without modulation) for an extra
 400ms, eliminating the squelch tail at the receiving end.

 A/B Buttons:
 Assign functions to A and B buttons: Channel Group Select,
 High/Low Power, Talk-Around, Alphanumeric Channel Name
 or Accessory.
#
:page
#

 DTMF SelCall/Channel Name Display:
 Select display of either DTMF SelCall or Channel Name
 on the main screen.

 Power-on Display:
 Select power-on display of either Channel Number or
 SelCall/Channel Name (as selected above).

 2-Tone Group:
 Select either Group 1-2-3, Group 4-5-6 or custom tone(
 based on the location of PROM) decoding tone system.
#
#
#
:edit_environm
#
      [Hardware Environment Common Parameter Editing]

 CAUTION: Do not change Hardware Environment Parameters
 unless you are certain that the transceiver hardware is
 suitable for the new value.

 Editing these parameters requires that this program be
 started with the -p switch on the command line. Otherwise
 the settings can only be viewed.

 Use the Arrow Keys to select the parameter to edit. Then use
 the Space Bar or Backspace Key to toggle among all possible
 settings for that parameter (except the numeric "IF" entry).
 When all parameters are set as desired, press the Esc Key to
 return to the Common Data Editor.

#
:page
#

 "IF" (Intermediate Frequency) entries are made with the
 0 - 9 keys and Backspace Key. Press the Enter Key to accept
 your entry, or Esc to cancel.
#
#
#
:edit_enviro
#
		 [Default Split Editing]

 "Default Split" is the freq difference between Rx Freq and
 Tx Freq, i.e. Rx Freq + Default Split = Tx Freq. Use the
 0 - 9, '-' keys and Backspace Key to set the desired freq.
 Press the Enter Key to accept your entry, or Esc to cancel.

 When it is set as desired, press the Esc Key to return to
 the Common Data Editor.
#
#
#
:make_dir
#
          [Make a new subdirectory on current Path]

 Input the name of the new subdirectory (up to 8 characters),
 then press Enter. Press Esc or F8 to cancel if you change
 your mind.

 Backspace: Delete character to left of cursor
 Delete:    Delete character above cursor
 Insert:    Toggle Insert/Overwrite mode (Insert is default,
	   Overwrite has block cursor)
#
#
#
:remove_dir
#
       [Remove the named (or pointed-to) subdirectory]

 Only empty child directories from the current subdirectory
 can be removed. In the file list, the inverse box can be
 positioned with the arrows to select a subdirectory to
 remove, by pressing F4. If the file list selection is not
 a subdirectory, a box will appear in which you can enter
 the name of the subdirectory to be removed. This name must
 be the same as one of the subdirectories displayed in the
 file list, and any files in that subdirectory must have
 already been deleted.

 Backspace: Delete character to left of cursor
 Delete:    Delete character above cursor
 Insert:    Toggle Insert/Overwrite mode (Insert is default,
	   Overwrite has block cursor)
#
#
 
